We’ll just overload the constructor (or add a second param) so we can inject a `RateTable` that already knows about weekend rates. Inside the `ParkingLot` ctor we keep the same signature:

```java
public ParkingLot(List<Floor> floors,
                  PricingStrategy pricingStrategy,
                  SpotAllocationStrategy allocationStrategy) {
    this.floors = floors;
    this.pricingStrategy = pricingStrategy;
    this.allocationStrategy = allocationStrategy;
}
```

When we build the `TimeBasedPricing` we’ll pass the same `RateTable` instance, and its `calculate` will call `rateTable.getRate(vehicleType, entryTime)` (or `getWeekendRate` if needed). So the only change is in the wiring code that creates the `RateTable`/`TimeBasedPricing` and hands them to `ParkingLot`; the `ParkingLot` ctor itself stays unchanged.
